           'Aya’s city retains fabrication technology but changes how people obtain its output. Citizens gain access through merits for useful tasks or through reputation, with fame affecting resources and privileges. The resource limits are real: freeing people from the old mental conditioning increases demand. This is a distinct arrangement from the earlier New Pretty cohort, because social visibility now becomes an alternative route to material access. It is not unlimited abundance.\n\nThe control estimate is cautiously positive: residents can seek resources through their own actions and changing public attention, yet influential people gain disproportionate leverage over a centralized allocation system. The sources do not establish equal ownership or an independent machine sovereign. They also do not settle whether every obscure resident retains a secure minimum of food, shelter and care, so the guarantee coordinate remains unresolved. Useful projects, journalism and other work continue, whether paid in merits or rewarded with attention. No population-wide nonworking share follows from the reputation mechanism. The regime label is therefore provisional.',
